  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C347-norte",
      "sequence": 6270,
      "title": "el norte — north, an English word living in Spanish",
      "headword": "el norte",
      "romanization": "el norte",
      "gloss": "north — Germanic northa through Old French nord; this is literally the English word north, borrowed back into a Romance language",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:fc4e39f5481fffaa",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say office, then station. (La oficina; la estación.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "¿dónde está? — the question this answers."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el país — the thing this often divides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "NOR-te, two beats with the weight on the first. The r is a single tap against the ridge behind your teeth. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el norte — \"the north.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Almost every word you have met so far came down from Latin. This one did not."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin's word for the north was septentriō, the seven plough-oxen — the seven stars of the Great Bear, wheeling around the pole. It is a beautiful word and Spanish barely uses it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What Spanish uses instead is Germanic. Northa was the north in the mouths of the sailors who worked the Atlantic and the North Sea, and their four direction words travelled with their trade: into Old French as nord, and from there into Spanish as norte."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So norte and English north are not cousins that drifted apart. They are the same word, and the -e on the end is the only thing Spanish added to make it pronounceable."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Names remember it. Norway is the north way; a Norman was a north-man."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of the four points, and a reminder that Spanish borrows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el norte",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el norte*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el norte del país",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el norte del país*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la estación está al norte",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la estación está al norte*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Is norte Latin or Germanic? (Germanic.) And what did Latin's own septentriō name? (The seven plough-oxen, the stars of the Great Bear.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C347-sur",
      "sequence": 6280,
      "title": "el sur — south, the sunward quarter",
      "headword": "el sur",
      "romanization": "el sur",
      "gloss": "south — Germanic sunthaz through Old French sud; the same word as English south, and probably built on the word for sun",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:ade9373ac78d9705",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say station, then north. (La estación; el norte.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el país — what a direction cuts in half."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la ciudad — what sits in one half or the other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "SUR, one beat, and the r at the end is a single tap rather than a roll. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el sur — \"the south.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Like norte, this came in with Atlantic sailors rather than down from Rome. Germanic sunthaz became Old French sud, and Spanish took it as sur. It is the same word as English south."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is inside it is a guess, but a good one: sun. Stand anywhere in northern Europe and the sun is never overhead — it swings across the southern half of the sky, all day, all year. The sunward quarter is the south, and so the sun gave its name to a direction."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish has both shapes. Sur on its own, and sud- when it joins onto another word, as in Sudamérica."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English does the same trick and never notices: south, but southern with a different vowel, and Sussex for the South Saxons."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two of the four points, and both of them Germanic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el sur",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el sur*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el norte y el sur",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el norte y el sur*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la ciudad está al sur",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la ciudad está al sur*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which everyday thing is probably hiding inside sur? (The sun.) And which shape does Spanish use when the word joins onto another? (Sud-.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C347-oeste",
      "sequence": 6290,
      "title": "el oeste — west, named after the evening",
      "headword": "el oeste",
      "romanization": "el oeste",
      "gloss": "west — Germanic westaz through Old French ouest; the same word as English west, and behind it the old root for evening that also gives vespers",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:114edd753019e57d",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say north, then south. (El norte; el sur.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "este — a word that will trip you here."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "¿dónde está? — the question all four points answer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o-ES-te, three beats with the weight on the second. The o and the e stay separate, each with its own beat. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el oeste — \"the west.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The third Germanic borrowing in a row: westaz to Old French ouest to Spanish oeste. Again the same word as English west."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Under it lies an old root meaning evening. Latin took that root as vesper, the evening star and then the evening itself, which is why the evening prayers of a monastery are called vespers. So the west is not named for a place. It is named for an hour — the quarter of the sky the sun falls into at the end of the day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now the trap. The fourth point, the east, is el este — and este is already in your mouth as the pointing word for this. The two have nothing to do with each other. The pointing este is Latin iste, that one of yours. The compass este is Germanic, a cousin of English east, from a root meaning dawn."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two words that fell into the same shape by accident. The article tells you which is which: el este is a direction, plain este is a finger."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three points named and the fourth explained, with a warning attached."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el oeste",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el oeste*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el norte y el oeste",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el norte y el oeste*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el pueblo está al oeste",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el pueblo está al oeste*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What hour is oeste named for? (The evening.) And are the compass este and the pointing este the same word? (No — a pure accident of shape.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C347-centro",
      "sequence": 6300,
      "title": "el centro — the centre, and the spike it turns on",
      "headword": "el centro",
      "romanization": "el centro",
      "gloss": "centre, downtown — Latin centrum from Greek kentron, the spike a compass turns on; English centre, central, concentrate and eccentric all turn on it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:4468114bc17bb38a",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say south, then west. (El sur; el oeste.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la plaza — what usually sits in it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la ciudad — what has one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "SEN-tro, two beats with the weight on the first. The c before e is soft, and tr runs together. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el centro — \"the centre,\" and in a town, \"downtown.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek kéntron was a goad — the spiked stick a farmer used to drive oxen. From the spike came the second sense: the fixed leg of a pair of compasses, the point you stick into the paper so the other leg can swing round it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is what a centre is. Not the middle in some vague way, but the place where the point went in."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin took it as centrum and geometry has used it ever since. English has centre, central, concentrate (to drive things toward one point), eccentric (out of centre, off the spike) and the epicentre of an earthquake."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In everyday Spanish, el centro is where you go to shop and to meet people — the old middle of a town, the part built first, with the plaza and the church in it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The four points of the compass, and the still place they turn around."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el centro",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el centro*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el centro de la ciudad",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el centro de la ciudad*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la iglesia está en el centro",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la iglesia está en el centro*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was a kéntron first? (A goad, a spiked stick.) And what does eccentric mean, word for word? (Out of the centre, off the spike.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C347-alrededor",
      "sequence": 6310,
      "title": "alrededor — around, and the wheel inside your knee",
      "headword": "alrededor",
      "romanization": "alrededor",
      "gloss": "around, round about — al plus rededor, from Latin retro backwards crossed with rota, a wheel; English rotate, rotund and retro all roll here",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e586776a4a1b7f0f",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say west, then the centre. (El oeste; el centro.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la rodilla — a little wheel, and a cousin of this."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la plaza — the sort of thing you stand in the middle of."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "al-rre-de-DOR, four beats with the weight on the last. The r after l is rolled, not tapped. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "alrededor — \"around,\" \"round about.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Take it apart: al + rededor. The second piece is an old Spanish word for the space surrounding a thing, and it comes from Latin retrō, backwards, pulled sideways by rota, a wheel."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both ideas are in the word. Something alrededor is behind you as well as in front, because it has gone all the way round."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Rota is generous. English gets rotate, rotation, rotary, rotund and roll from it, and Spanish gets a surprise: la rodilla, the knee, is Latin rotella, a little wheel — the joint your leg turns on."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Retrō gave English retro, retrograde and retrospect."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the space around you and the knee you stand on are both named for turning."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five ways to say where: north, south, west, the middle, and everything encircling it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "alrededor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *alrededor*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "alrededor de la plaza",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *alrededor de la plaza*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la gente está alrededor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la gente está alrededor*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does Latin rota mean? (A wheel.) And which part of your body is a little wheel in Spanish? (La rodilla, the knee.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "findings": []
